---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Kisspeptin: Reproductive Research, No Approval — Nawtch"
  description="Kisspeptin has real Phase 1/2 fertility and endocrinology trials, but no FDA approval for any consumer use — mechanism and status."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/kisspeptin/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>Kisspeptin</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">A hormone the body makes naturally, under real reproductive-endocrinology study — not approved for any consumer use.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent3); background:rgba(240,160,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent3);">Tier 2 — Investigational / Not Currently Approved</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Kisspeptin has genuine, small human clinical trials behind it, but it is <strong>not FDA-approved for any use</strong>. This page describes its mechanism and
            the actual reproductive-endocrinology research — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a recommendation to use it.</strong> Talk to a doctor before
            considering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Kisspeptin is a naturally occurring peptide hormone that the body itself produces in
            the hypothalamus, where it acts as a master upstream signal for reproductive hormone
            release — it stimulates GnRH (gonadotropin-releasing hormone) neurons, which in turn
            drives LH and FSH release from the pituitary. This upstream role is what makes it
            interesting for reproductive-medicine research: rather than replacing a downstream
            hormone directly, it triggers the body's own natural signaling cascade.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Research Actually Exists</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Kisspeptin has a real, if early-stage, human trial base concentrated in reproductive
            endocrinology and fertility research — a narrower and more clinically-supervised
            research context than most Tier 2 compounds. Small Phase 1/2 trials have studied it as
            a potential trigger for oocyte maturation in IVF (as an alternative to hCG, with early
            signals of a lower risk of ovarian hyperstimulation), and as a treatment to help
            restore menstrual cycles in women with hypothalamic amenorrhea, typically delivered via
            a subcutaneous pump or intravenous/subcutaneous bolus under close monitoring. This is
            legitimate clinical research — but it remains investigational, run in supervised
            trial settings for specific reproductive conditions, not evidence supporting any
            unsupervised consumer or fitness use.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/ebiom/article/PIIS2352-3964(25)00133-1/fulltext" target="_blank" rel="noopener">eBioMedicine — Intranasal Kisspeptin Administration Rapidly Stimulates Gonadotropin Release in Humans →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Regulatory Status</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Kisspeptin is <strong>not FDA-approved for any indication</strong> and is not
            available as a prescription drug. It exists purely as a research compound studied in
            controlled academic and clinical-trial settings — it is not currently part of the
            FDA's compounding-substance review process that BPC-157, TB-500, and related peptides
            have gone through, and there is no pending approval decision to track for it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11006622/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMC — Kisspeptin: A Potential Therapeutic Target in Metabolic and Reproductive Dysfunction</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
